title: Embedded metadata
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: overview
aliases: mfr_meta exif tags id3
summary: Reconcile reads the metadata inside media files — tags, EXIF, stream properties — into mfr_meta_* fields, with memory-safe parsers and a per-repository map.

Many formats carry metadata about their ''content'': an audio file's artist, album and
title, an image's capture date, camera and GPS position, a video's title. Unlike the stat
fields, which describe the filesystem entry, these describe the payload. Reconcile
extracts a curated, normalised subset into reserved `mfr_meta_*` fields, queryable like
any other:

```
mf -n music metarecord -q 'mfr_meta_artist = "Nina Simone" AND mfr_meta_date > @"1960-01-01"' get
same(mfr_meta_artist, mfr_path = "/music/a.flac")
```

The parsers are ''pure Rust and memory-safe'', in the daemon process:

* audio — `lofty`: ID3v2/ID3v1, Vorbis comments (FLAC, Ogg, Opus), MP4/M4A atoms, tags ''and'' stream properties (duration, bitrate, sample rate, channels);
* images — `nom-exif`: the EXIF/TIFF tags of JPEG, TIFF and common RAW formats — capture date, make and model, orientation, GPS, dimensions;
* video containers (Matroska, MP4) — best effort: title, and dimensions and duration where cheap.

''Why no sandbox here'', when the GUI confines every media decoder
([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]): a memory-safe parser fed a crafted file can at worst
panic or return wrong values, never execute code, unlike the C decoders behind `ffmpeg`
or the WebView. So each backend runs under `catch_unwind` — a panic is a warning naming
the file, its partial output dropped (without it, one image whose EXIF makes `nom-exif`
hit an `assert!` on file-controlled sizes failed every reconcile of the repository) — and
its allocations are bounded (lofty's allocation limit; for `nom-exif`, the file's real
length). A richer `exiftool`/`ffprobe` backend would need that sandbox in the daemon
first, and is out of scope until then.

What is extracted is up to each repository ([[The metadata map]]); when it happens:
[[When metadata is extracted]].

